Log — Day 1
1. Actual duration: 1h — compilation pipeline + pointer exercise.

2. New tools/commands: gcc -E/-S/-c, -Wall, -Wconversion, nm, file, grep -n, echo $?.

3. Bug + root cause: undefined reference to main — real cause: I was editing/compiling the wrong file, not a C syntax error.

4. Hypothesis tested and corrected: none formal this day (more exploratory/setup).

5. Confidence at checkpoints: medium — solved the pointer-as-output exercise correctly, but didn't work through the return sum reflection on my own until prompted.

6. Open questions (resolved now): -Wconversion didn't flag anything on the double→int conversion of return sum because the value (36.0) was exact and fit within the 0-255 exit code range — a coincidence of value, not a correct design. main is still semantically wrong, returning sum instead of a status code; left as is, a conscious decision not to fix it now.

7. Connection to the project: first contact with "pointer as a function output mechanism" — a pattern that will keep reappearing when passing signal buffers by reference in C++/DSP.

8. Source of help: Copilot to write the code after understanding the goal; own research for the compilation pipeline (gcc man/--help).

Additional reflection: I used Copilot to generate the code on both days after understanding what I needed to do. Worth questioning how much of the logic I actually formulated vs. how much I just validated — this matters for knowing whether I can defend this code in a technical interview without assistance.
